<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rukun Negara</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1970">1970</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rukun Negara ('Principles of the Nation') is Malaysia's national philosophy, proclaimed on 31 August 1970 after the racial riots of 13 May 1969 to encourage unity among its peoples. Its five principles are belief in God, loyalty to king and country, the supremacy of the constitution, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rule-of-law">rule of law</a>, and courtesy and morality.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rukun-negara.jpg" alt="Rukun Negara" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vzach · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TuguNegara.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>On 13 May 1969, three days after a general election in which opposition parties gained at the expense of the ruling coalition, racial riots broke out, mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuala-lumpur">Kuala Lumpur</a>. The government's report counted 196 dead; Western diplomatic sources suggested close to 600. A state of national emergency was declared, and for the next 18 months the country was run by the National Operations Council (MAGERAN), which suspended newspapers and parts of the constitution. In January 1970 it set up the National Consultative Council (Majlis Perundingan Negara), drawn from ministers, state governments, parties, the press, trade unions and religious and professional groups, to propose ways of building national unity.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>At its second meeting the council discussed a draft prepared by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of National Unity and formed a committee on the Rukun Negara under Tun Tan Siew Sin, with members including Ghazali Shafie, Syed Hussein Alatas and Datuk Harun Idris. Its first meeting, on 31 March 1970, proposed seven Pillars of the Nation: belief in God, loyalty, justice, citizenship, the integrity of the nation, well-being and decency. The committee's revised draft was passed by the National Operations Council on 13 August 1970 after some changes. The principles have been compared with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>'s Pancasila.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The Rukun Negara was proclaimed by the fourth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yang-di-pertuan-agong">Yang di-Pertuan Agong</a>, Ismail Nasiruddin of Terengganu, at the Independence Day celebration at Dataran Merdeka, on that occasion renamed National Day to promote unity. On 2 September 1970 a meeting decided that the Malay text was the original and the others translations, and recitation was encouraged at official events and the opening of Parliament and the state assemblies. The Ministry of Information distributed brochures and pocket editions and produced films. Schools recite the pledge weekly after the national anthem, Negaraku, and on 8 December 2005 the government made its reading compulsory at official functions.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rukun Negara” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The racial riots of 13 May 1969 in Kuala Lumpur, which officially killed 196 people, led to the suspension of parliament, rule by a National Operations Council under Tun Abdul Razak, the national ideology of <b>Rukun Negara</b> and the New Economic Policy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malaysia">Malaysia · Into the Present</a></li></ul></section>
